            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Portuguese for the edition, then the Portuguese for the article."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: o facto",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "o facto — \"the fact\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "É um facto: ninguém viu nada. It is a fact: nobody saw anything."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In Brazil: o fato, which in Portugal is a suit. De facto — in fact."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "facto is a learned borrowing from Latin factum \"a deed, a thing done\" (EtymDB), the past participle of facere \"to do, to make\"; the inherited form of the same word is feito."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
